Xpost from the user's side
=========================

Xpost is an interpreter for the PostScript language, written in C and
distributed under the BSD 3-clause licence. The whole of it is a library,
libxpost; the `xpost` program is a small application over that library
(src/bin/xpost_main.c), and src/bin/xpost_client.c is a smaller example
of embedding it.

Beside the interpreter the tree builds a second, independent library,
libxpost_dsc, which reads a document's structuring comments -- the
`%%`-prefixed metadata a conforming document carries -- into a header
and a table of per-page offsets, without executing the document. Two
programs sit over it: `xpost_dsc file.ps` prints what the comments say
(version, title, page count, bounding box, and where each page starts),
and `xpost_view file.ps` is a viewer that uses the page offsets to
render one page at a time, built only where a window system is
available (X through xcb, or Windows).

It implements PostScript LanguageLevel 3, including the graphics: paths,
paint, colour spaces, clipping, images, patterns, shadings, halftones,
and text through host faces found by fontconfig and rendered by
freetype, through Type 3 fonts described in PostScript, and through
Type 42 fonts carrying a TrueType program in /sfnts. COMPLIANCE marks
each operator implemented, partly implemented or not, and closes with
the behavioural deviations and the implementation limits; doc/COMPAT
says where each user-visible name comes from.

How much is tested is a fair question to ask of an interpreter. The
suite is 432 tests, run at both of the object widths the tree builds at;
doc/GATING.md says which run answers which question. Beside it are
corpora of real programs, fetched rather than committed, rendered and
compared page by page (tests/corpus/README.md). tools/coverage.sh
reports how much of the C sources a full run executes, one report per
object width, and is explicit that executing a line is not the same as
pinning down what it does.


== Getting the source ==

  git clone https://github.com/luser-dr00g/xpost.git
  cd xpost


== Building ==

Meson is the build the tree is developed and released against, and the
one every CI lane uses. It needs meson 1.0 or later, ninja, and a C
compiler.

  meson setup build
  ninja -C build

leaves the interpreter at build/src/bin/xpost, runnable from where it
was built: an xpost started out of a build tree finds the PostScript
files it boots from under the source tree's data directory, so nothing
has to be installed to try a change to them.

  ninja -C build install

installs the program, the library and the data files. `meson setup
-Dprefix=/somewhere build` chooses where.

Every library it uses is optional and sought rather than required, so a
build with none of them present still produces an interpreter. What each
one buys:

  libpng                 the png and pngalpha devices
  libjpeg                the jpeg device
  freetype, fontconfig   scalable fonts, and finding them by name
  xcb                    the xcb window device, on X11
  zlib                   the FlateDecode and FlateEncode filters, and
                         with them the compressed streams pdfwrite puts
                         in a PDF

`meson configure build` lists the options an already configured build
took. The one worth knowing about is `-Dlarge-object=true`, which widens
the fields of the PostScript object; the tree is built and tested both
ways and the narrow build is the primary one.

The tree also carries an autotools build (`./autogen.sh`, `make`). It
builds the same programs, but it is not what CI builds and not what the
tree is developed against, so meson is the one to reach for.


== Running a program ==

  xpost [options] [file.ps]

`xpost` below is the installed program or the one in the build tree,
whichever is to hand:

  build/src/bin/xpost -d png -o page.png examples/dancingmen.ps

`-d` names the device the page is painted into and `-o` names the file
it is written to. A page is letter unless the program says otherwise or
`-g` does; `-g` wants the whole geometry and refuses less, so it is
`-g 2550x3300+0+0` rather than `-g 2550x3300`. `xpost --help` lists
every option and every device the build was configured with.

A run of several pages needs a name per page, and the output name says
where the page number goes:

  xpost -d png -o page%d.png   job.ps    page1.png, page2.png, page3.png
  xpost -d png -o page%03d.png job.ps    page001.png, page002.png, ...

`%d` writes the number as it stands and `%0Nd` writes it in N digits
with leading zeros, N being one digit from 1 to 9; a number too large
for the field is written whole rather than cut, so a run longer than the
field was written for goes on numbering its pages. One name carries one
of them. A name spelling a page number some other way -- `%2d`, `%-5d`,
`%.2d`, `%010d` -- is refused where the page is written rather than
taken as text, because a name taken as text puts every page of the run
in one file and says nothing about it. The same name is what a program's
`/OutputFile` sets, and it is read the same way there.

Two things about a run are worth knowing before the first one, and both
turn on the same question: whether there is somebody at the other end of
it. There is, when the standard input is a terminal and no output file
was named -- naming one with `-o` says the run is something waiting for
that file rather than somebody typing.

A run with somebody there is a session. It opens with a banner and a
copyright notice on the standard output, is offered the interactive
executive after the named program ends (see below), and announces each
page: at the end of one the interpreter prints `----showpage----` on the
standard output and reads a line from the terminal, so it waits there
until it is given one. That is the announcing behaviour PLRM 2.4.4
describes for interactive use.

A run with nobody there gets none of it. Its standard output carries
what its program printed and nothing else, so a run whose answer is read
by a script is read as it stands: `xpost -V` prints the version on a
line of its own, and a program's output arrives with nothing of the
interpreter's around it. A program embedding the library chooses its own
page behaviour through xpost_create.

The transcripts below are of runs at a terminal where the transcript is
about a session, and of runs with the standard input redirected
otherwise.

Two of the examples under examples/ draw pages:

  xpost -d png -o men.png   examples/dancingmen.ps   figures from a
                                                     Sherlock Holmes story
  xpost -d png -o sin1x.png examples/sin1x.ps        a function plotted by
                                                     bezier interpolation

The examples are part of the source tree and are not installed;
`ninja -C build install` installs only the data files the interpreter
boots from.


== The devices ==

A device is what a page is painted into and written out as. `-d NAME`
selects one; `xpost --help` prints the list this build actually has,
which is shorter where an optional library was missing at configure
time.

  pgm        grayscale raster, binary portable graymap (P5)
  ppm        colour raster, binary portable pixmap (P6)
  pbm        bilevel raster, binary portable bitmap (P4); greys reach it
             through the halftone in force
  tiff       colour raster, TIFF; one strip, LZW-compressed to a file
             and uncompressed to the standard output
  png        colour raster, PNG
  pngalpha   the same with an alpha channel, so the paper is transparent
  jpeg       colour raster, JPEG

  pdfwrite   PDF
  dscwrite   PostScript, conforming to the Document Structuring
             Conventions
  svgwrite   SVG

  bbox       draws nothing and reports the bounding box of what would
             have been drawn
  null       executes the drawing and discards it

  raster     hands the page back to an embedding program as a raw
             framebuffer
  bgr        the same, in a fixed byte order
  record     the class that holds a page as the marks that made it,
             rather than as pixels

  xcb        an X11 window (a build on X11)
  gdi, gl    a window on Windows

The two paginated container formats, pdfwrite and dscwrite, hold every
page of a run in one file; a name carrying a page number selects a file
per page instead. Nothing else can hold more than one page in a file, so
for the rasters and for svgwrite a page number gives a file per page and
a name without one keeps the last page, every page having rewritten the
one file.

A page-device parameter the specification defines and this interpreter
does not provide -- /Duplex, /NumCopies, /Collate, the media selection
keys and the rest -- is a request it cannot satisfy. What becomes of one
is the /Policies dictionary's answer, itself a page-device parameter:
the standing answer ignores the parameter and carries the rest of the
request through, and a program that would rather be told writes

    << /Duplex true /Policies << /Duplex 0 >> >> setpagedevice

which raises configurationerror on a device that cannot duplex. Naming a
device this interpreter cannot make is refused that way whether or not
the request asks for it. A key the specification does not define is not
one of these: it is a device feature, and reaches the device as one.

The compiled codecs take four tuning keys, asked for with
setpagedevice like any other device feature and accumulated the same
way -- later requests on the same device merge over earlier ones, and
switching OutputDevice starts from the new device's defaults:

  /png_compression_level  how hard the png codec compresses, 0 to 9;
                          unset is 3 (png and pngalpha)
  /png_interlaced         1 for an interlaced image, written in seven
                          passes, so the page is held whole (png and
                          pngalpha)
  /png_filter             which row filters the png codec may choose
                          between: (adaptive) -- unset; the library's
                          whole menu, chosen per row -- (none-sub-up)
                          or (none); a word outside the three is
                          refused with that roster
  /jpeg_quality           the jpeg codec's quality scale, 0 to 100;
                          unset is 90

A default for any of them can be set for the whole run from the
command line with `-p key=value` (repeatable), which records it on the
device class exactly as an embedding program does through
xpost_dev_png_options_set() and xpost_dev_jpeg_options_set(); a
program's own request still overrides either. The switch refuses a
key this build's devices do not read, naming the keys that exist, and
a value outside its key's range or vocabulary, in the key's own
terms; `xpost --help` lists the keys the build takes.

A colon after the device name introduces a mode. On raster it is the
byte order a lent framebuffer is in -- `raster:rgb`, `raster:argb`,
`raster:bgr`, `raster:bgra` -- and those four are the whole of it: a
word outside them is refused before the run begins, naming what was
given and the four, because a page lent back in an arrangement nobody
asked for is read as though it were in the one that was. A selection
naming no format at all gets rgb. Everywhere else the mode is how much
of a page is held at once, which is the next section.


== Big pages, and holding one a band at a time ==

A page's raster costs its width times its height whatever is drawn on
it, and at large sizes that is what a run runs out of. Six devices can
take their page a band at a time instead -- pgm, ppm, pbm, tiff, png
and jpeg -- and hold one run of rows rather than the whole page. What
is held in place of it is the marks, so a big page costs what its
drawing costs rather than what its paper costs. Every other device
holds the whole page, the alpha PNG device among them: what a banded
page is held as is the marks made on it, and the transparent paper
that device clears to is not a mark.

A page small enough to fit in one band is held whole, so this costs a
small page nothing and there is nothing to turn on. A device may be
given a mode after a colon to say otherwise:

  xpost -d pgm       -o page.pgm job.ps    banded where banding pays
  xpost -d pgm:whole -o page.pgm job.ps    the page held whole
  xpost -d pgm:band  -o page.pgm job.ps    the page held as its marks

The bare name is the only one of the three that is weighed. The other
two say which route they want at any page, so the three are compared
by changing one word.

What :band settles is that the page is held as its marks whatever its
size. How deep a band is still comes from the budget, so a page the
budget covers is held in one band that covers it, and CurBandHeight
reads 0 there just as it does for :whole. What bands an ordinary page
is a smaller budget, below.

A mode outside those two words is refused before the run begins,
naming what was given and what the device takes. It has to be: a
selection carrying any mode at all is one the weighing leaves alone,
so pgm:bnad accepted would turn the weighing off without saying so. A
device whose page cannot arrive a band at a time takes neither word,
so bgr:band is refused too. raster:FORMAT is a different selector on a
different device and still means the pixel format.

The rule has no exceptions. A device that takes no mode refuses every
word rather than only the ones it has heard of, so bgr:zzz is refused
where bgr:band is. Nothing downstream reads a mode it does not
recognise, so a word accepted here would be a misspelling that changed
nothing and said nothing, which is the one way a selection can be
wrong and leave no trace of it.

  -d record   selects the recording class itself. It names no device
              to play into, so it plays into the colour raster, which
              makes it the same as -d ppm:band. It takes no mode.

Nothing in a page description chooses any of this. PLRM Appendix G
calls banddevice an obsolete LanguageLevel 1 device setup operator
that should never be used in a page description, so a program asks for
a grayscale page with /OutputDevice /pgm and how its marks are held is
settled by whoever runs the interpreter.


== How deep a band is ==

  -b, --band-bytes=BYTES

How deep a band is comes from a byte budget divided by what one row of
the device's raster costs. The budget is four million bytes unless
this says otherwise, and that covers the sheets a page is normally cut
to, so those are painted directly: letter and A4 and A3 in colour all
read CurBandHeight 0.

The same number decides whether a page is held as its marks at all. A
page the budget covers arrives in one band, which is the page, so it
is painted on the device the run asked for and nothing is written
down. So lowering the budget is the way to band a page that would
otherwise be painted whole, and it is the only way: :band asks for the
recording route and not for a second band, and a page under the budget
takes that route and still arrives whole.

  xpost -d pgm --band-bytes=100000 -o page.pgm job.ps

bands a letter page at the default resolution into 163-row bands,
because a row of it is 612 bytes. The same run at 612 gets one-row
bands. A budget that will not buy a single row of the page in hand is
refused when the device is made, naming the budget and what a row
costs, rather than quietly taken as one row; and a budget that is not
a whole number of bytes from 1 to 2147483647 is refused before the run
begins, naming what was given and the range.

The budget bounds the marks as well as the raster: see below.


== Where a banded page's marks go ==

  -s, --spill=auto|never|always

  auto     in memory while they come to less than the raster banding
           the page saves and less than the band budget, and in a
           scratch file past either. This is the default
  never    in memory whatever they come to, touching no scratch file
           at all. What the page costs then follows its drawing with
           no limit
  always   in a scratch file from the first mark

The scratch file has no name, nothing else can open it, and it goes
when the run does.

Two bounds under auto rather than one, because they answer for
different pages. What banding saves is the tighter of the two for a
page whose bands save little, and it says nothing at all about a page
whose bands save nothing -- a page held whole has no raster its marks
could cost more than, which is what a page reached with :band under
the budget is. The band budget answers for that page and for every
other, so a page held as its marks costs a band of raster and a
budget's worth of marks whatever is drawn on it.

Under auto and always, whether a scratch file can be made is settled
when the run starts, by making one and writing to it -- a permissions
check would say yes for a directory on a full filesystem, for one a
system-call filter refuses, and for one mounted read-only. Under
always a run that cannot have one is refused there with an ioerror.
Under auto the run is told once, naming the directory and the reason
the system gave, and goes on; a page that then needs to spill holds
its marks in memory instead. That message goes out at the log's error
level, so -q does not gate it: -q sets the interpreter's own quiet
flag and does not touch the log level at all.

Under never nothing reaches the scratch directory, not even to look,
which is one of the reasons the state exists -- a sandbox forbidding
file creation wants it. What is being chosen is unbounded memory.
Nothing else ever selects never: a scratch directory that refuses a
file does not demote a run to it.


== What a program can ask about it ==

currentsystemparams reports four names. All four are reported and none
can be set, for the reason above.

  MaxBandBytes    what one band of a page may cost this run, in bytes
                  of raster held at once: the budget --band-bytes set,
                  or four million where it set none

  CurBandHeight   the rows the device now being painted into holds of
                  its page at once, and 0 where it holds the page
                  whole

  RecordSpill     what this run asked for: /never, /auto or /always

  CurRecordSpill  where the page in hand actually has its marks:
                  /memory, /file, or /refused, meaning in memory
                  having been refused a file it wanted

Two names for the spill rather than one, because they can differ: a
run that asked to be weighed, on a machine that will take no scratch
file, reports auto and refused, and a reader given only the first
could not tell that from a page that never needed to spill.

The two band names are a limit and what it bought, and reading them
together is how a run's banding is checked: MaxBandBytes divided by
what one row of the raster costs is CurBandHeight, and a budget that
buys every row of the page reads 0 for the second, meaning the page
was held whole. At the default budget a letter sheet at 300 dpi in
grey, which is 2550 by 3300, reads 1568; a 612 by 40000 strip in
colour reads 2178; and the ordinary sheets read 0.


== The interactive session ==

PLRM 2.4.4 describes an interactive executive: a prompt that reads a
statement from the standard input, executes it, and prompts again.
Xpost offers one where there is somebody to type at it -- that is,
where the standard input is a terminal -- either with no program named
at all, or after the named program has ended.

  $ build/src/bin/xpost
  Xpost 0.0.1
  Copyright (C) 2013, Michael Joshua Ryan. All rights reserved.
  Licensed under the BSD 3-clause licence, without any warranty;
  the file COPYING states the terms.
  PS>

Type `quit`, or send an end of file (ctrl-D, or ctrl-Z on Windows), to
leave.

The prompt carries a digest of the operand stack between `<` and `>`,
which is one character per object: a digit for a small integer, `S` for
a string, `D` for a dictionary, `}` for a procedure, and so on
(data/prepr.ps has the table). So an empty stack prompts `PS>` and one
holding 7, 8 and a procedure prompts `PS<78}>`.

A statement is read whole, however many lines it takes. While a
procedure, a string or a hexadecimal string is still open, each newline
brings a sub-prompt naming the constructs waiting to be closed, one
character each, followed by `.:`

  PS>{
  {.:(
  {(.:)
  {.:}
  PS<}>pstack
  {(\n)}
  PS<}>

Nothing else reads the standard input while the session has it, so a
program that reads its own data from the file it is in cannot be run
through the session; run it as a named file instead. PLRM 2.4.4 says
the same, and adds that a program-generating application should never
invoke `executive`.


== The example programs ==

The example programs live under examples/, one directory for all of
them. Every .ps file there is a program to run; the `teapot` file is
mesh data that tea1.ps and teamath.ps read, so run those two from the
examples directory. The test suite runs every program the directory
holds (tests/run-examples-test.sh), so each of these runs cleanly
under the null device.

These use no graphics at all. Several read an array of strings named
ARGUMENTS, which they expect to be defined before they run; each of
those carries a %%SampleArguments: comment near its top naming values
it runs with:

  binshape.ps  vertical streams of 0 and 1; ARGUMENTS is the width
  digit.ps     draws its argument in seven-segment digits
  tab340.ps    the smaller golfed guitar tablature generator
  tab418.ps    the larger golfed tablature generator
  tabref.ps    the reference tablature generator
  hwbin.ps     hello world, written with binary radix numbers
  hwhex.ps     hello world, written as a hexadecimal string
  mat.ps       matrix and vector math routines
  ubf119.ps    the 119-character universal binary-function table
  ubf70.ps     the 70-character universal binary-function table

These draw with the graphics operators:

  arc.ps        approximate arcs with bezier segments, printing the
                points
  arcdist.ps    measure an arc's length with pathforall
  bitfont.ps    a non-caching Type 3 font using 8-bit images
  class.ps      a class system with inheritance, built from
                dictionaries
  dancingmen.ps figures from a Sherlock Holmes story
  matrix.ps     matrix and coordinate-system operators in PostScript
  sin1x.ps      a function plotted by bezier interpolation
  tea1.ps       a spinning teapot, one page per frame until
                interrupted
  teamath.ps    project the teapot mesh through a viewing matrix
  testdraw.ps   pages of random arcs, pies, lines, curves and boxes

A program whose %%Unbounded: comment says so runs until interrupted.

The programs that take no argument run as they are:

  xpost examples/hwhex.ps

For the rest, define ARGUMENTS and then run the program by name. From
the examples directory:

  $ cat >tab.ps <<'EOF'
  /ARGUMENTS [ (A) (Bm) (F#m) (F#m) ] def
  (tab340.ps) run
  EOF
  $ xpost tab.ps
  e 0---2---2---2---
  B 2---3---2---2---
  G 2---4---2---2---
  D 2---4---4---4---
  A 0---2---4---4---
  E 0---2---2---2---

`-DNAME=TOKEN` defines a name in userdict from the command line, which
is enough for a number or a string but not for the array these want:
the value is one token.


== PostScript arithmetic and function-calling ==

Just a brief review of what you should already know if you're trying
to use xpost. PostScript uses a Reverse-Polish Syntax where the
operator follows the operands.

  a + b      Infix Notation (ALGOL, C)
  ( + a b )  "Cambridge" Polish Notation (Lisp)
  a b +      Reverse Polish Notation (RPN)

PostScript does not use punctuation symbols for the operators,
but short words.

    add sub mul div
    idiv mod            2 operand
    atan exp

    cos sin sqrt
    ln log              1 operand
    abs neg
    floor ceiling
    truncate round

note:
%<-- PostScript comments begin with '%' and run till the end of line -->$

For example, if you define /a, /b, and /c to contain values for
the variables in the formula "ax^2 + bx + c",

    /a 25 def   % define the variables a b and c
    /b 10 def
    /c 3 def

and a definition for x,

    /x 12 def

you can calculate the value of the formula like this:

                   % stack picture:
    a x x mul mul  % ax^2
    b x mul        % ax^2 bx
    c   add   add  % ax^2+bx+c

which for those values is 3723.


== Stack manipulation ==

To turn this formula into a function, we need to take the input
for the function and define it as x before applying the formula.

    % x  quadratic  ax^2+bx+c
    /quadratic {
        /x exch def    % define x from stack
        a x x mul mul  % calculate quadratic as above
        b x mul
        c add add
    } def

When the function is entered, the input value is on the operand stack.

    number
   |------|

Then we push the (literal) name /x.

      /x
    number
   |------|

Unfortunately, `def` expects them to be in this order:

    number
      /x
   |------|

Hence `exch` is used to reorder the top two elements of the stack.
When reading a PostScript sequence like `/name exch def` one way to
think of `exch` is like a pronoun whose antecedent is just below
the name.

    whatev  /x  exch  def
       ^         |
        ---------

The function so far isn't very general. So let's tease out a, b, and
c and make them parameters, too, instead of hard-coded names.

    % a b c x  quadratic  ax^2+bx+c
    /quadratic {
        /x exch def   % a b c   % define x
        3 1 roll      % c a b   % reorder elements
        x mul         % c a bx  % calculate the bx term
        3 1 roll      % bx c a  % reorder elements
        x x mul mul   % bx c ax^2  % calculate the ax^2 term
        add add                    % sum up
    } def

This brings us to `roll`. As you can see from the stack comments,
`3 1 roll` permutes three elements of the stack. The first
number is how many elements to manipulate. And the second number
indicates how many elements to move and in which direction.

    /a /b /c   % a b c
    3 1 roll   % c a b
    3 -1 roll  % a b c
    3 2 roll   % b c a
    3 3 roll   % b c a
    3 0 roll   % b c a
    3 2 roll   % c a b
    3 -1 roll  % a b c


    n j roll

    __ j > 0 __     move top j elements to the bottom of n

    n            TOS
     -------------|
    |       j     |
    |        -----|
    |       |     |
    V       V     |
    a b c d e f g h
   ^       |       |
   |       |-------|
   ^           |
    -<-<-<-<-<-


    __ j < 0 __   move j elements from the bottom of n to the top

    n            TOS
     -------------|
    |     j       |
    |-----        |
    |     |       |
    V     V       |
    a b c d e f g h
   |       |       ^
   |-------|       |
       |           ^
        ->->->->->-

    "Positive J to roll away", "Negative to bring 'em back".

So the next step in revising/optimizing our function is to remove the /x name
as well. Without a name for x, the copy of it each term needs has to be
fetched off the stack, which is what `index` does: `n index` pushes a
copy of the element n below the top.

  % a b c x  quadratic  ax^2+bx+c
  /quadratic {
      4 2 roll     % c x a b     % reorder elements
      2 index mul  % c x a bx    % copy x and multiply
      4 1 roll     % bx c x a    % reorder elements
      1 index mul  % bx c x ax   % copy x and multiply
      mul          % bx c ax^2   % multiply
      add add                    % sum up
  } def

  25 10 3 12 quadratic ==
3723

The two `index` counts differ because the two terms are reached from
different places: after the first `roll` the top is b and x is two
below it, and after the second the top is a and x is one below.

If we apply `bind` to the procedure, all the operator names will
be replaced by the operators themselves, and the function now
performs no dictionary lookups at all.

  /quadratic load bind ==
{4 2 --roll-- 2 --index-- --mul-- 4 1 --roll-- 1 --index-- --mul-- --mul
-- --add-- --add--}

`==` breaks its output at 72 columns, which is where that second line
comes from.

This function will now execute as quickly as possible,
and it will not interact with the current namespace
and it does not allocate any data.


== Arrays ==

It should be apparent from the last example that a procedure is
just an array, wrapped in curly braces rather than square brackets.

So we could construct the same procedure in the following way.

  /quadratic [
  4 2 /roll load 2 /index load /mul load
  4 1 /roll load 1 /index load /mul load
  /mul load /add load /add load
  ] cvx def

This is similar to the way bind constructs its bound procedure,
by loading the operator objects by name and embedding them in
the array. One must be careful in using the direct method shown
here. It works for a name whose value is an operator object;
a name whose value is a PostScript procedure lands in the array
as the procedure itself, and an executable array nested inside
another is pushed rather than executed.

Nearly everything in systemdict here is an operator object. Of its 401
entries 356 are operators; 20 hold a procedure, 2 hold a literal array
(the two standard encodings), and the rest are standard data such as the
page sizes and $error. Every operator the language defines is here, and
none of the interpreter's own machinery is -- that lives in an internal
dictionary off the dictionary stack -- so systemdict is the whole
language and nothing else, and userdict and globaldict start empty. The
caveat therefore bites rarely; which side of it a name falls on can be
asked outright:

  /add load type    %  operatortype
  /=print load type %  arraytype

`=print` is one of the 20, so this prints nothing and leaves both the
string and the procedure behind:

  [ (hi) /=print load ] cvx exec

and this prints it:

  [ (hi) /=print load /exec load ] cvx exec

You see the convenience of bind. It performs all those loads for you,
but leaves the procedure invocation intact.

  /quadratic {
      4 2 roll 2 index mul
      4 1 roll 1 index mul
      mul add add = } bind def
  /quadratic load ==

{4 2 --roll-- 2 --index-- --mul-- 4 1 --roll-- 1 --index-- --mul-- --mul
-- --add-- --add-- --=--}
